@page "/docs/registry/registry-item-json"
<PageTitle>Registry item reference - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Item reference</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">An item is one installable unit: everything the CLI needs to add it and to undo it later. The same object appears twice, in the manifest you write and in the JSON <CmdChip Command="blaizio build" /> serves, with file contents inlined in the second.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Fields</h2>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                    <BzTableHead>Field</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>What it does</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>$schema</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Optional, and written into every built item by <CmdChip Command="blaizio build" />: <code>https://blaiz.io/schema/registry-item.json</code>. Items inside a manifest leave it out, since the manifest carries its own.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>name</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Required. The slug the item is installed and tracked by, and the file name in the built output.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>type</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>What the item installs. Defaults to <code>registry:ui</code>. See the table below.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>title</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A human label for search results and gallery listings.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>version</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The version this document describes, in the registry's own scheme. Lets consumers pin an install (<code>blaizio add item@1.2.0</code>) and records what landed in <code>blaizio.json</code>. Omit it and the registry is unversioned.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>description</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>One line about the item, shown by <CmdChip Command="blaizio search" /> and <CmdChip Command="blaizio view" />.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>author</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Who published the item, e.g. <code>Jane Doe &lt;jane@@acme.dev&gt;</code>. Display only - shown by <code>view</code> and <code>docs</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>categories</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Free-form browse tags (<code>forms</code>, <code>charts</code>, ...). <code>search --category</code> filters on them, case-insensitively.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>docs</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A short note shown when the item installs and by <code>docs</code>/<code>view</code>: setup steps, a documentation URL, a caveat. The one message an author can put in front of the consumer at install time.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>meta</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Arbitrary registry-defined metadata, carried through untouched. The CLI never reads it - it exists for your own tooling and UIs.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>nugetDependencies</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Packages installed with <code>dotnet add package</code> before the files land. A bare id resolves the latest version; <code>Id@1.2.0</code> pins one. Two items pinning different versions of the same package fail the add with an explicit error.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>devDependencies</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Packages needed only at development/build time (analyzers, source generators). Installed like <code>nugetDependencies</code>, then marked <code>PrivateAssets="all"</code> in the csproj so they never flow to the app's own consumers. A package another item lists as a runtime dependency stays runtime.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registryDependencies</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Other items pulled in first, resolved transitively. Plain names come from the same registry; <code>@("@ns/item")</code> references reach another one.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>files</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The source files the item carries. Required for everything except themes and fonts.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>cssVars</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Token overrides, split into <code>light</code> and <code>dark</code> maps. The payload of a theme item; any item may carry one.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>css</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>CSS blocks the item ships into the consumer's tokens file - keyed by block prelude (<code>@@keyframes spin</code>, <code>@@utility tab-4</code>, <code>@@layer components</code>, a selector), value = the block body. Written inside a managed, item-keyed region; a re-install replaces it and <code>remove</code>/<code>uninstall</code> strip it. Token values belong in <code>cssVars</code>, never here - the token contract stays canonical.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>font</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The font a font item applies. <code>name</code> is a catalog font, <code>heading</code> chooses the heading face over the body one.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>Unknown keys are ignored rather than rejected, so a manifest written against a newer vocabulary still installs on an older CLI; the fields it does not understand are simply not acted on.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Types</h2>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                    <BzTableHead>Type</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>Installs</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:ui</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A styled component. Its files are copied into the consumer's output folder and rewritten to their namespace.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:lib</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Shared helper files other items depend on: utilities, extensions, converters.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:theme</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Token overrides, no files. The <code>cssVars</code> payload is patched into the consumer's tokens file.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:font</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A font selection, no files. Patches the font overlay and wires the webfont link in the host page.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:file</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Loose project files: config, services, assets. Each file's <code>target</code> is required and project-root-relative (<code>~/wwwroot/robots.txt</code>) - anywhere inside the project, never outside it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:page</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Routable pages. Without a <code>target</code> a file lands in the project's pages folder (<code>Components/Pages</code> when it exists, else <code>Pages</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:template</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A whole-app template, scaffolded by <CmdChip Command="blaizio new" /> (never <code>add</code>). Its files <em>are</em> the project layout, csproj included, written with <code>@("{{RootNamespace}}")</code>/<code>@("{{ComponentNamespace}}")</code>/<code>@("{{ProjectName}}")</code>/<code>@("{{Skin}}")</code> substituted; <code>registryDependencies</code> name the component set installed after wiring, and its NuGet dependencies install like an item's.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>Every destination is contained: whatever a manifest says, a file resolves strictly inside the consumer's project - a component under the output folder, a <code>~/</code> target under the project root.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Files</h2>
        <p>Each entry carries a <code>path</code> relative to the manifest and a <code>type</code>. A <code>registry:ui</code>/<code>registry:lib</code> file lands at its path minus the item-type prefix, under the consumer's configured output folder; <code>target</code> overrides that, relative to the same folder.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ItemTarget" />
        <p>A <code>registry:file</code>/<code>registry:page</code> file targets the <em>project root</em> instead: write the target with the <code>~/</code> prefix. Installed paths are recorded exactly as written, so <code>remove</code> and <code>uninstall</code> take loose files back out too.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ItemUniversal" />
        <p>In the built output each entry gains a <code>content</code> string. That is the only difference between the manifest form and the served form, and it is why one request per item is enough to install it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Token payloads</h2>
        <p><code>cssVars.light</code> patches the consumer's <code>:root</code> block and <code>cssVars.dark</code> their <code>.dark</code> block, declaration by declaration: everything else in their tokens file survives. Names may be written with or without the <code>--</code> prefix, so <code>"primary"</code> and <code>"--primary"</code> are the same token.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ItemTheme" />
        <p>Stay inside the token contract the components already read. Inventing a new variable name means nothing consumes it, and re-applying a preset later will not know about it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">What install records</h2>
        <p>Every install is written into the consumer's <code>blaizio.json</code>: the item name (qualified with its namespace when it came from one), the files it wrote, the packages it added, and the items it pulled in as dependencies. That record is what makes <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove" /> and <CmdChip Command="blaizio uninstall" /> exact rather than best-effort, so an item that is honest about its files and dependencies is also an item that uninstalls cleanly.</p>
    </section>
</div>
